# Setup Terraform for AWS EKS

This plan outlines the steps to build the Infrastructure as Code (IaC) required to run your Helm chart on a production-grade Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) cluster.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **AWS Account Required**: To apply this Terraform code later, you will need an active AWS account, AWS CLI installed, and configured credentials (`aws configure`). If you don't want to actually deploy it (to avoid AWS charges), we can still write the code to learn how it works!

## Proposed Changes

We will create a new `terraform/` directory with a modular configuration. We will use the official, well-maintained Terraform AWS modules for the VPC and EKS cluster to follow industry best practices.

### terraform/

#### [NEW] [provider.tf](file:///c:/Users/nikhi/Desktop/devops/devops-crud-app/terraform/provider.tf)
Configures the AWS provider, sets the default region (e.g., `us-east-1`), and specifies the required Terraform versions.

#### [NEW] [variables.tf](file:///c:/Users/nikhi/Desktop/devops/devops-crud-app/terraform/variables.tf)
Defines variables like the cluster name, region, and node instance types to make the configuration reusable.

#### [NEW] [vpc.tf](file:///c:/Users/nikhi/Desktop/devops/devops-crud-app/terraform/vpc.tf)
Uses the `terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws` module to provision:
- A new VPC
- 2 Public Subnets (for the Ingress Controller/Load Balancers)
- 2 Private Subnets (for your application Pods and Nodes)
- NAT Gateways for outbound internet access

#### [NEW] [eks.tf](file:///c:/Users/nikhi/Desktop/devops/devops-crud-app/terraform/eks.tf)
Uses the `terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws` module to provision:
- The EKS Control Plane
- Managed Node Groups (the EC2 instances where your pods will run)
- Required IAM roles and policies

#### [NEW] [outputs.tf](file:///c:/Users/nikhi/Desktop/devops/devops-crud-app/terraform/outputs.tf)
Outputs the cluster endpoint and the command needed to configure your local `kubectl` to connect to the new cluster.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Verification
- Run `terraform fmt` to ensure the code is formatted correctly.
- Run `terraform validate` to ensure the syntax and module configurations are valid.

### Manual Verification
- Run `terraform plan` to see the exact AWS resources that would be created.
- (Optional) Run `terraform apply` to actually provision the cluster in AWS.
